First, determine how many protons are in the body.

- Mass of the person = 62 kg  
- Half of the mass is protons → \(m_{\text{protons}} = 0.5 \times 62 \text{ kg} = 31 \text{ kg}\)

The mass of a single proton is \(m_p \approx 1.673 \times 10^{-27}\,\text{kg}\).

\[
N_{\text{protons}} = \frac{31\,\text{kg}}{1.673 \times 10^{-27}\,\text{kg}}
                    \approx 1.85 \times 10^{28}\text{ protons}
\]

---

### Decay rate

The proton half‑life is \(t_{1/2}=10^{33}\) yr.

The mean lifetime is

\[
\tau = \frac{t_{1/2}}{\ln 2}
      \approx \frac{10^{33}\text{ yr}}{0.693}
      \approx 1.443 \times 10^{33}\text{ yr}
\]

Convert \(\tau\) to days:

\[
\tau_{\text{days}} = 1.443 \times 10^{33}\text{ yr} \times 365 \text{ d/yr}
                   \approx 5.27 \times 10^{35}\text{ days}
\]

The decay rate per day is

\[
\frac{N_{\text{protons}}}{\tau_{\text{days}}}
  = \frac{1.85 \times 10^{28}}{5.27 \times 10^{35}}
  \approx 3.5 \times 10^{-8}
\]

---

**Answer: A. 3.5 × 10⁻⁸**

